Z||000000||maryjane||11-25-2001||01:23 AM||||Alright, got your attention:<BR>Not counting the problems stemming from 9-11-01, what's the problem with the economy? <P>Nasdaq has doubled in points this year<BR>Dow is within 1100 points of it's record high & has had 3 straight weeks of gains.<BR>The Fed (Greenspan) has been lowering interest rates to all time lows.<BR>Average hrly wages are still up at record values<BR>Oil, thus gasoline are approaching 5 year lows.<P>But..unemployment has risen over 1 full % point in the last 6 months. People are hesitant to buy big ticket items.<BR>Industry growth/expansion is stagnant.<BR>Some analysts say we are in recession. If the stock market was driving the US economy in the '90s, what is driving it today? Is it better to have a runaway market with stocks overvalued as much as 1000%price/earnings, or as it is now, with stocks priced /share closer to what they actually are earning? Is the media partly responsible? Who is? What's in the future for 2002? Any economists/financial wizards out there?||||reg||1
