Z||000006||Raydar||11-25-2001||02:29 PM||||Lots of tech and telecom companies are still laying off. Telecom is still kind of slow. Some of the layoffs are due to slow build-out of infrastructure. Others are to "cut costs" for the benefit of the stockholders.<P>I wonder how all the stockholders are going to feel when their companies shudder to a halt because all of the experienced employees have been laid off or retired, and the entire workforce is "entry level".<BR>Bellsouth is a case in point. <BR>Tried to get anything done, lately? Rotsa Ruck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||11-25-2001||03:02 PM||||Nasdaq-3yr chart:  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/nasdaq.gif">  <P><BR>the Dow Chart-3yr chart:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/big.gif">  <P>As you can see, except for the Sept 11th drop, the dow is not really that bad off, and increasing. The Nasdaq, on the other hand has not come anywhere near it's yr 2000<BR>high. Remeber when all the tech company's stocks were selling for astronamical prices?<BR>The companies themselves were just barely treading water, the stock was WAY overvalued, in relation to what the companies were actually earning, or what the stock was yeilding in dividends. Investors were wildly buying stock, thinking they would make fortunes on it. Any new company offering a new stock on the market saw their stock price double, triple, even quadruple in a matter of days. This is what is known as a bull market, it tends to drive an economy. But it is a false economy, built on speculation. Some buyers did make a lot of $, if they sold while it was still high. Many of these companies now are banckrupt, or losing money. Most were so-called .coms. <BR>Stocks are now closer to their real value, but the enthusiasim is gone, because everyday people got burned by over confidence, un-wise investing. Industry also went wildly about it's expansion fueled by the false economy, creating jobs, services and products for which there was no real market. Now we are in reality, and companies have had to drastically downsize and layoff employees by the thousands. Productivity is still strong, goods and services still about the level of yr-2000. Interst rates are way down, so eventually, a new upswing will gain some momentum, but caution will be the watchword for investors. I believe the news media not only reports on the market & economy, but also steer it by their bright newscasts during booms, and their constant doom & gloom during less profitable times. Rather than report it in common everyday terms, they bring in wall street analysts that report in a typical wall street jargon, that most of us don't understand. We remain puzzled, thus cautious, or depressed. In the real world, not the on-paper world, saleable production is what should fuel an economy. In a prduction-based economy, only one thing matters-productiion.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||11-25-2001||03:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Lots of tech and telecom companies are still laying off. Telecom is still kind of slow. Some of the layoffs are due to slow build-out of infrastructure. Others are to "cut costs" for the benefit of the stockholders.<P>I wonder how all the stockholders are going to feel when their companies shudder to a halt because all of the experienced employees have been laid off or retired, and the entire workforce is "entry level".<BR>Bellsouth is a case in point. <BR>Tried to get anything done, lately? Rotsa Ruck.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Raydar: Yoou can't expect a corp to continue to employee people at any wage when there is little or no market for their goods or services. The US auto market in the '70s is a prime example of that. Failure to recognize the wants & needs of the consumer. <BR>Using out of date expensive, and labor intensive manufactoring technques. Correcting these errors results in keeping a more reliable economy overall. It's why even in a slow economy, the amt of production available is still there. You can do more, with less expenditures.  ||||reg||1
