Z||000010||maryjane||11-25-2001||11:20 PM||||First of all, it takes years for anything that is done by govt to actually affect jobs and inflation. Consequently, anything that happens during one administration can be traced back directly to the previous administration. The 'surplus' was paid into the government by employees's taxes of companies that were falsely<BR>footed. The companies ran rampant. Wide open, until they were forced to downsize because of their own weight. It simply was not sustainable, by any govt. You cannot out-produce your customer base forever. These are NOT my personal opinions, they are easily proven facts. Ask any economist. I wish one person would explain to me what ANY administration really does to influence the economy. No government can create and maintain jobs for their people for very long. There simply isn't a demand for that much production.<BR>The Bush  and Reagan administrations both had to work under liberal congresses. If anyone is to blame it would have to lie with congress. Inversely, Clinton had a predominantly Republican congress. You can't give credit to the liberal pres for good things, and not include his conservative congress. Nor can you blame conservative presidents, and ignore the fact that the congress was liberal. I probably make as little as any 'little guy' you can point out. I got a tax cut. It is not the governments place to help the little guy. In modern economies, it is governments job to encourage industry, so they can provide employment to the little guy. If you took all the $ in the US and split it evenly among the millions of Americans, in 1 yrs time, the same people that had the $ originally, would have it again. Unless of course, you are in favor of doing it every Saturday night.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> The liberal administration of the 90's was well aware of the danger of the runaway stockmarket-fueled by speculation and the .coms. For their own political reasons, they let it run, hoping to win the 2000 election before reigning it in by increasing interest rates. As it turned out, they lost the election, the market collapsed under it's own weight and the liberals are left with only the emptiness of saying"hey, we had it good for awhile". Had the economy continued on it's 90's course, inflation would have reared itself out of the limits of manageability. You would have BOTH high unemployment AND high inflation. The absolute worst scenario. You have little money, and goods cost so much the $ won't buy much. As it stands now, unemployment is at 5.4% and little inflation, with the economy on a slow incline of growth. <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||11-26-2001||09:47 AM||||please dont confuse the stock market with 'investment' in businesses.<P> after the intial IPO if you by a companies stock they get NONE of that money.<P> The part of the stock the company still holds goes up in value if the stock price goes up - bu that is on paper only - they cant trade it in for real money - if they started selling the stock they hold in any quanities the price would crash.<P> If you dont get in on the IPO then you are NOT investing in the company - you are gambling of what the price of the stock will be tomorrow - nothing more.||||reg||1
