Z||000014||maryjane||11-27-2001||12:37 AM||||Ken, I agree with you about the initial stock offering being the only way businesses make anything directly off of stocks. But many people in the late 90's-early 2000 jumped on the ipo's wildly, multiplying the on paper worth of the fledgling dot.coms. Many of these new companies looked on these increases in their stock prices as a barometer of where their business sales would go, continuing to expand, when in fact there was little to expand for. A lot of investment firms, and corporations that should have put their money into their own business invested heavily into these company's stocks also, and encouraged their people to. They were all trying to make an easy, quick profit, instead of relying on thier own business savvy to become more efficient, more profitable. <P>RayB, Neither the Clinton administration, or any other has ever "gave" anybody a job. (Monica Lewinski,civil service, military, and Civilian Conservation Corps excepted) Business and industry provides jobs, not govt. Free rides are over buddy, you get what you earn. If you want to encourage people to wait for the govt to take care of them, go ahead. This country is just now recovering from the excessive spending the last social experiment caused. 'The Great Society'. It spawned decades of welfare dependant families. There is no utopia, it's a myth that has disproven itself. Want long-term growth? Stability? Encourage business. The Soviet Union tried your philosophy for over 50 years. The result was a nation of poor people and a obselete, ineffecient industrial base. They're history. China went down the same road, until recently. Now that they have encouraged business and industry, they are an emerging power, with a stabilized economy. I say again, govt does not give jobs.   ||||reg||1
